.SH MODES
.TP
\fBrift\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIA\fR \fIB\fR
Writes a unified diff to standard output, as \fBdiff \-u\fR does.
\fBpatch\fR(1) applied to \fIA\fR gives \fIB\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-summary\fR
Writes no diff. Prints the line and byte count of each file, the number
of segments that are equal, differing or approximate, the first 10
differing ranges (counted from 0, end excluded), the moved blocks, the
peak heap and the time.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR, \fBrift tui\fR \fIA\fR \fIB\fR
Opens a terminal view of the diff. The view is usable before the diff is
complete. A hunk still being refined shows as one pending row and fills
in by itself. The keys are:
.RS
.TP
\fBn\fR, \fBp\fR
Jump to the next and previous difference.
.TP
\fBspace\fR, \fBb\fR
Page down and up.
.TP
\fBg\fR, \fB:\fR
Go to a line of \fIA\fR.
.TP
\fBm\fR
Jump from the first moved block on screen to its other end.
.TP
\fBq\fR
Quit.
.RE
.IP
\fBrift tui\fR is \fBrift \-i\fR. With fewer than two arguments after
\fBtui\fR, it is a file name. When standard input or output is not a
terminal, it exits with status 2 before it reads the files.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fISPEC\fR
Compares records by key and ignores their order. Prints one line per
key that differs, with tabs between the fields and line numbers counted
from 1. \fB~\fR marks a key in both files with different records, \fB\-\fR a key only in
\fIA\fR, \fB+\fR a key only in \fIB\fR. \fIA\fR and \fIB\fR are the line where the record starts,
or \fB\-\fR when it is absent:
.RS
.nf
~\fIKEY\fR	\fIA\fR	\fIB\fR
\-\fIKEY\fR	\fIA\fR	\-
+\fIKEY\fR	\-	\fIB\fR
.fi
.RE
.IP
\fISPEC\fR is one of:
.RS
.TP
\fBcsv:\fIN\fR
CSV column \fIN\fR, from 1.
.TP
\fBcsv:\fINAME\fR
CSV column with the header \fINAME\fR.
.TP
\fBjson:\fIPATH\fR
Dotted field path in NDJSON records, such as \fBuser.id\fR.
.TP
\fBcopy:\fINAME\fR
Column \fINAME\fR of the \fBCOPY\fR blocks of a \fBpg_dump\fR file.
.RE
.IP
The key is printed as the file has it, quotes included. A tab, line
break or backslash in a key prints as \fB\\t\fR, \fB\\n\fR or
\fB\\\\\fR. A record counts as unchanged only after its bytes compare
equal. A key that occurs twice in one file stops the run with exit status
2, possibly after changes were printed. The message on standard error
then says the output is incomplete and must be discarded, so check the
exit status before you use the output. \fB\-\-key\fR cannot be combined
with \fB\-\-summary\fR or \fB\-\-interactive\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-stats\fR
After a diff, prints to standard error the peak heap, the time, the
number of approximate regions and the moved blocks. With \fB\-\-key\fR it
prints the peak heap, the time, the number of passes over the files and
the number of records in each file. With \fB\-\-summary\fR it adds
nothing, since the summary already prints the peak heap and the time.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-moves\fR
Skips the search for moved blocks. The unified diff is byte for byte the
same; the search only adds notes to \fB\-\-summary\fR, \fB\-\-stats\fR
and the terminal view, which then say that it was off. It cannot be
combined with \fB\-\-key\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mem\fR \fISIZE\fR
Sets the heap limit. The default is 256M. The smallest limit that works
depends on the files. Below it, Rift exits with status 2 and a budget
error that ends with a limit to rerun with, such as
\fBrerun with \-\-mem 10333K\fR. It is not a lower bound, and a run at
that limit can still stop at a later stage and name a higher one. In key
mode a larger limit means fewer passes over the files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit status follows \fBdiff\fR(1).
.TP
\fB0\fR
The files are identical.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The files differ.
.TP
\fB2\fR
An error: an unreadable file, a budget too small, a duplicate key, an
invalid option or \fB\-i\fR in a build without the terminal view.
The message goes to standard error.
.SH MEMORY
The heap stays at or under \fB\-\-mem\fR. Rift reserves each allocation
on the data path from the budget before it makes it, and reads files with
\fBpread\fR(2) into fixed buffers. It does not map files into memory. The
test suite checks the peak with a counting allocator.
.PP
These are not guaranteed:
.IP \(bu 2
The resident set size. It also holds the binary, thread stacks and the
page cache the kernel counts against the process.
.IP \(bu 2
A minimal diff. Like patience diff, the output can show a change as
larger than the smallest possible one.
.IP \(bu 2
Detection of every moved block. Blocks are found from sampled lines, so
some moves are missed.
.SH APPROXIMATE REGIONS
When a region is too large to refine within the budget, Rift replaces it
whole: the output is still correct, but coarse. This happens in large
regions with no line that is unique in both files, such as long runs of
identical lines.
.PP
The unified diff marks such a hunk with the word \fBapprox\fR after the
closing \fB@@\fR of its header, where \fBdiff \-p\fR prints a function
name. \fBpatch\fR(1) ignores it. The terminal view draws the hunk in
yellow with a \fB~\fR. \fB\-\-stats\fR counts the approximate segments
and lines.
.SH MOVED BLOCKS
A block of at least 3 lines that appears in both files at different
positions is reported as moved. The unified diff still shows it as a
deletion and an insertion. \fB\-\-summary\fR and \fB\-\-stats\fR list the
blocks, and the \fBm\fR key in the terminal view jumps to the other end
of one. When the budget leaves no room for the search, the output says so.
.SH GIT
\fBrift git\-diff\fR is the command git runs for an external diff. Git
passes it \fIpath old\-file old\-hex old\-mode new\-file new\-hex
new\-mode\fR, and for a rename or copy also the new path and git's
rename lines. Rift prints a unified diff with git's headers
(\fBdiff \-\-git\fR, mode lines, \fBindex\fR, \fB\-\-\- a/\fIpath\fR,
\fB+++ b/\fIpath\fR, and \fB/dev/null\fR for an added or deleted file)
and exits 0 whether or not the files differ, because git treats any other
status as a failure and stops with status 128. The status of
\fBgit diff\fR is git's own: 0, or 1 with \fB\-\-exit\-code\fR when there
are differences. Rift exits 2 on an error. Options such as
\fB\-\-mem\fR go before the arguments git adds.
.PP
To use it for large files only, name them in \fB.gitattributes\fR and
define the driver. Git runs the command through the shell, so a quoted
string can carry options:
.PP
.RS
.nf
*.sql diff=rift
*.csv diff=rift

git config diff.rift.command 'rift git\-diff \-\-mem 1G'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The headers match \fBgit diff\fR, except that the hunk headers carry no
function name, the hash of a work tree file is written as 0000000
because git does not pass one, and hashes are always 7 characters.
.PP
A file that is a Git LFS pointer on either side is replaced by the object
in \fB.git/lfs/objects\fR of the common git directory, found with
\fBgit rev\-parse \-\-git\-common\-dir\fR, and read in place. If the
object is missing Rift names its oid and suggests \fBgit lfs fetch\fR.
If its size differs from the pointer's, Rift stops. Rift does not
check the hash, and ignores \fBlfs.storage\fR.
.PP
Git writes every blob that is not in the work tree to a temporary file
before it runs the command. For a file that is not stored with LFS, that
is a full copy: on a 10 GB file, 10 GB of disk and the time to write it.
Comparing a work tree file with the index has one such copy; comparing two
commits has two.
.PP
For the terminal view, use \fBgit difftool\fR. It passes the two files as
\fB$LOCAL\fR and \fB$REMOTE\fR and does not resolve LFS pointers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git config difftool.rift.cmd 'rift \-i "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"'
git config difftool.prompt false
git difftool \-t rift
.fi
.RE
.SH SERVE
\fBrift serve\fR [\fB\-\-max\-session\-mem\fR \fISIZE\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-total\-mem\fR \fISIZE\fR] [\fB\-\-stats\fR] speaks Rift's
protocol, JSON\-RPC 2.0 framed as in LSP, on standard input and output,
for editors and other programs. A client opens a pair of files as a
session, then asks for counts, pages of differing segments and moved
blocks, and the lines of one segment on both sides. Every answer has a
size bound that does not depend on the files. The protocol is described
in \fBdocs/protocol.md\fR in the source repository.
.PP
\fB\-\-max\-session\-mem\fR (default \fB1G\fR) is the most memory one
session may ask for; a session gets 256 MiB when it does not ask.
\fB\-\-max\-total\-mem\fR (default \fB2G\fR) bounds the heap of the whole
server: the sessions share it, less 8 MiB the protocol keeps for its
buffers. At most four sessions are open, and none is closed for being
idle. \fB\-\-stats\fR prints the peak heap to standard error on exit.
.PP
The server exits with status 0 when standard input closes, and 2 when
it cannot start. With a file argument after it, \fBserve\fR is a file
name: \fBrift serve other\fR diffs a file called \fBserve\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a patch and apply it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift a.sql b.sql > changes.patch
patch a.sql changes.patch
.fi
.RE
.PP
Browse the differences:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-i a.sql b.sql
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count the differences without writing a diff, with a 1 GiB heap limit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-summary \-\-mem 1G a.csv b.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write a patch and report the peak heap and time on standard error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-stats a b > changes.patch
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compare two CSV files by the column \fBid\fR, whatever the row order:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-key csv:id a.csv b.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compare NDJSON by a nested field, and a \fBpg_dump\fR by a COPY column:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rift \-\-key json:user.id a.ndjson b.ndjson
rift \-\-key copy:id a.sql b.sql
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBdiff\fR(1), \fBpatch\fR(1)
